---
// The header's live readout: where FI99 is, and what time it is there.
//
// This fills the void between the logo and the nav. That slot is chrome, and
// chrome carries state, not content — everything here is either true-forever
// (city, coordinates) or genuinely live (date, clock), and none of it is a
// message. A permanently visible element that says something reads as a
// billboard; one that reports something reads as an instrument, which is the
// register the rest of the site is already in.
//
// The clock runs on our time, not the visitor's. Showing someone their own
// clock tells them nothing they don't know; showing them ours says where we
// are and whether we're plausibly awake. Naming the IANA zone rather than a
// fixed offset is also what keeps the EST/EDT suffix correct across DST
// without a table of dates to maintain.
//
// Toronto because that is where all three of us are — see people.js. Changing
// cities is this object and nothing else.
const place = {
  label: 'TORONTO',
  zone: 'America/Toronto',
  lat: '43.65°N',
  lon: '79.38°W',
};

// The dashes are the no-JS state, and they are deliberate. A static build can
// only bake in its own build time, so rendering a real clock here would ship a
// number that is confidently wrong and stays wrong. Dashes say "live readout,
// not running", which is true. Each placeholder matches its value's character
// count so the mono grid never reflows when the script fills them in.
---

<!-- Hidden below lg on purpose. Base.astro's nav comment records that four
     items already crowd the header at 320px; there is no room to buy back on
     phones, and a cramped readout is worse than none. -->
<div
  class="hidden select-none items-center gap-3 px-6 font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.2em] lg:flex"
  data-readout
  data-zone={place.zone}
  transition:persist="readout"
>
  <span class="text-bone/55">{place.label}</span>

  <!-- Coordinates are the first thing to go when the gap narrows: they are the
       most ornamental value here, and the least missed. -->
  <span class="hidden text-bone/20 xl:block" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
  <span class="hidden text-bone/35 xl:block">{place.lat} {place.lon}</span>

  <span class="text-bone/20" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
  <span class="text-bone/40" data-readout-date>----------</span>

  <span class="text-bone/20" aria-hidden="true">·</span>

  <!-- aria-hidden: a value that rewrites itself every second is noise to a
       screen reader and there is no reading of it a listener benefits from.
       The city and coordinates above stay exposed — that is the part carrying
       information, and it never changes.

       flex, so the whitespace between these spans collapses and the spacing is
       stated rather than inherited from source formatting: the seconds must
       butt straight up against the minutes, and the zone must not. -->
  <span class="flex items-center" aria-hidden="true">
    <span class="text-bone/55" data-readout-time>--:--</span><span
      class="text-bone/30"
      data-readout-secs>:--</span
    >
    <!-- Dimmest of the three because it is the only part that moves. Putting
         the fastest-changing digits at the lowest contrast is what keeps a
         permanently visible clock from pulling at peripheral vision. -->
    <span class="ml-[0.7em] text-bone/40" data-readout-zone>---</span>
  </span>
</div>
